1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,275
This is Mexico City.

2
00:00:01,275 --> 00:00:03,947
With more than twenty million people in its

3
00:00:03,947 --> 00:00:05,100
metropolitan area,

4
00:00:05,100 --> 00:00:07,590
it's one of the biggest cities on Earth.

5
00:00:07,940 --> 00:00:09,082
And it's sinking.

6
00:00:09,082 --> 00:00:10,160
Not by a little.

7
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:11,618
In some neighborhoods,

8
00:00:11,618 --> 00:00:14,536
the ground drops by tens of centimeters every

9
00:00:14,536 --> 00:00:15,360
single year.

10
00:00:15,710 --> 00:00:16,795
Churches lean.

11
00:00:16,795 --> 00:00:17,880
Streets crack.

12
00:00:17,880 --> 00:00:21,206
Old buildings that once stood at street level

13
00:00:21,206 --> 00:00:22,508
now sit below it,

14
00:00:22,508 --> 00:00:23,810
or rise above it.

15
00:00:24,160 --> 00:00:25,460
The strangest part?

16
00:00:25,460 --> 00:00:27,020
Five hundred years ago,

17
00:00:27,020 --> 00:00:29,360
most of this ground was underwater.

18
00:00:29,710 --> 00:00:32,090
So why is Mexico City sinking?

19
00:00:35,040 --> 00:00:36,710
By the end of this video,

20
00:00:36,710 --> 00:00:38,701
you'll see how an island city,

21
00:00:38,701 --> 00:00:39,728
a drained lake,

22
00:00:39,728 --> 00:00:42,104
a layer of soft clay and millions of

23
00:00:42,104 --> 00:00:44,930
thirsty people are pulling the ground down.

24
00:00:45,280 --> 00:00:48,311
Around twenty-two million people live in the Mexico

25
00:00:48,311 --> 00:00:49,710
City metropolitan area.

26
00:00:49,710 --> 00:00:52,683
That's more than the whole population of countries

27
00:00:52,683 --> 00:00:54,490
like Chile or the Netherlands.

28
00:00:54,840 --> 00:00:56,120
And it sits high up.

29
00:00:56,120 --> 00:00:58,680
The city center is about two thousand two

30
00:00:58,680 --> 00:01:01,240
hundred and forty meters above sea level,

31
00:01:01,240 --> 00:01:03,800
higher than many ski resorts in the Alps.

32
00:01:04,150 --> 00:01:05,395
At that altitude,

33
00:01:05,395 --> 00:01:06,778
the air is thinner.

34
00:01:06,778 --> 00:01:10,097
Each breath holds roughly a quarter less oxygen

35
00:01:10,097 --> 00:01:11,411
than at sea level,

36
00:01:11,411 --> 00:01:14,730
something visiting athletes notice immediately.

37
00:01:15,080 --> 00:01:17,030
It lies in a high valley,

38
00:01:17,030 --> 00:01:18,680
the Valley of Mexico,

39
00:01:18,680 --> 00:01:22,580
surrounded on all sides by mountains and volcanoes.

40
00:01:22,930 --> 00:01:25,655
To the south-east rises Popocatépetl,

41
00:01:25,655 --> 00:01:29,026
an active volcano more than five thousand four

42
00:01:29,026 --> 00:01:30,533
hundred meters high.

43
00:01:30,533 --> 00:01:31,608
On clear days,

44
00:01:31,608 --> 00:01:33,760
you can see it from the city.

45
00:01:34,110 --> 00:01:35,618
Here's the key fact.

46
00:01:35,618 --> 00:01:37,844
This valley is a closed basin.

47
00:01:37,844 --> 00:01:40,788
No river naturally drains it to the sea.

48
00:01:40,788 --> 00:01:43,660
Rain that falls here has nowhere to go.

49
00:01:44,010 --> 00:01:45,840
So for thousands of years,

50
00:01:45,840 --> 00:01:48,889
water collected at the bottom of the valley,

51
00:01:48,889 --> 00:01:51,193
forming a chain of shallow lakes.

52
00:01:51,193 --> 00:01:53,497
And as rivers washed in fine mud,

53
00:01:53,497 --> 00:01:55,530
it settled on the lake floor.

54
00:01:55,880 --> 00:01:57,616
Over thousands of years,

55
00:01:57,616 --> 00:02:00,185
that mud built up into a thick layer

56
00:02:00,185 --> 00:02:01,504
of very soft clay.

57
00:02:01,504 --> 00:02:02,892
Remember that clay.

58
00:02:02,892 --> 00:02:05,600
It's the hidden villain of this story.

59
00:02:05,950 --> 00:02:07,587
Long before the Aztecs,

60
00:02:07,587 --> 00:02:10,726
this valley was already home to great cities.

61
00:02:10,726 --> 00:02:11,613
Teotihuacan,

62
00:02:11,613 --> 00:02:13,319
north-east of the lakes,

63
00:02:13,319 --> 00:02:15,843
was one of the largest cities in the

64
00:02:15,843 --> 00:02:17,890
Americas around the year 500.

65
00:02:18,240 --> 00:02:20,199
There wasn't just one lake.

66
00:02:20,199 --> 00:02:23,137
A chain of connected lakes ran across the

67
00:02:23,137 --> 00:02:24,116
valley floor.

68
00:02:24,116 --> 00:02:26,285
The northern lakes were salty,

69
00:02:26,285 --> 00:02:28,033
while the southern ones,

70
00:02:28,033 --> 00:02:29,992
like Xochimilco and Chalco,

71
00:02:29,992 --> 00:02:33,000
were fed by springs and had fresher water.

72
00:02:33,350 --> 00:02:35,614
When the Spanish arrived in 1519,

73
00:02:35,614 --> 00:02:37,478
the largest of these lakes,

74
00:02:37,478 --> 00:02:38,410
Lake Texcoco,

75
00:02:38,410 --> 00:02:40,673
covered much of the valley floor.

76
00:02:40,673 --> 00:02:42,671
And in the middle of it was a

77
00:02:42,671 --> 00:02:43,070
city.

78
00:02:46,020 --> 00:02:47,734
According to tradition,

79
00:02:47,734 --> 00:02:48,591
the Mexica,

80
00:02:48,591 --> 00:02:51,233
the people we often call the Aztecs,

81
00:02:51,233 --> 00:02:54,089
founded their city on a small island in

82
00:02:54,089 --> 00:02:55,660
the lake around 1325.

83
00:02:56,010 --> 00:02:56,175
Their legend says the gods told them to

84
00:02:56,175 --> 00:02:56,323
settle where they saw an eagle on a

85
00:02:56,323 --> 00:02:56,356
cactus,

86
00:02:56,356 --> 00:02:56,421
eating a snake.

87
00:02:56,421 --> 00:02:56,574
That image is still in the center of

88
00:02:56,574 --> 00:02:56,660
Mexico's flag today.

89
00:02:57,010 --> 00:02:59,063
The island was a smart choice.

90
00:02:59,063 --> 00:03:00,586
It was easy to defend,

91
00:03:00,586 --> 00:03:03,567
and canoes could carry goods across the lake

92
00:03:03,567 --> 00:03:05,620
from every town on its shores.

93
00:03:05,970 --> 00:03:07,251
Its great market,

94
00:03:07,251 --> 00:03:08,319
at Tlatelolco,

95
00:03:08,319 --> 00:03:09,743
amazed the Spanish.

96
00:03:09,743 --> 00:03:13,730
Their chroniclers described tens of thousands of people

97
00:03:13,730 --> 00:03:16,720
buying and selling there on a single day.

98
00:03:17,070 --> 00:03:18,350
One Spanish soldier,

99
00:03:18,350 --> 00:03:19,934
Bernal Díaz del Castillo,

100
00:03:19,934 --> 00:03:22,432
later wrote that when they first saw the

101
00:03:22,432 --> 00:03:24,138
city rising from the water,

102
00:03:24,138 --> 00:03:26,880
some of them wondered if they were dreaming.

103
00:03:27,230 --> 00:03:28,261
To grow food,

104
00:03:28,261 --> 00:03:29,882
they built chinampas:

105
00:03:29,882 --> 00:03:33,566
narrow artificial islands made of mud and plants,

106
00:03:33,566 --> 00:03:36,733
piled up in the shallow water and anchored

107
00:03:36,733 --> 00:03:37,470
by trees.

108
00:03:37,820 --> 00:03:40,458
Chinampas were incredibly productive,

109
00:03:40,458 --> 00:03:42,332
with water always at hand.

110
00:03:42,332 --> 00:03:44,970
Some still exist today in Xochimilco,

111
00:03:44,970 --> 00:03:46,775
in the south of the city,

112
00:03:46,775 --> 00:03:49,760
and they are a UNESCO World Heritage Site.

113
00:03:50,110 --> 00:03:52,453
The city was connected to the shore by

114
00:03:52,453 --> 00:03:54,796
long causeways built across the water,

115
00:03:54,796 --> 00:03:57,379
with bridges that could be removed in case

116
00:03:57,379 --> 00:03:58,040
of attack.

117
00:03:58,390 --> 00:04:00,310
Part of the lake was salty.

118
00:04:00,310 --> 00:04:02,573
So the Aztecs built a long dike,

119
00:04:02,573 --> 00:04:04,699
more than ten kilometers long,

120
00:04:04,699 --> 00:04:07,921
to separate fresher water around the city from

121
00:04:07,921 --> 00:04:09,910
the salty water to the east.

122
00:04:10,260 --> 00:04:12,756
The dike was built in the mid fourteen

123
00:04:12,756 --> 00:04:13,396
hundreds,

124
00:04:13,396 --> 00:04:15,060
after a disastrous flood,

125
00:04:15,060 --> 00:04:18,900
and its design is traditionally credited to Nezahualcoyotl,

126
00:04:18,900 --> 00:04:21,780
the poet-king of the nearby city of Texcoco.

127
00:04:22,130 --> 00:04:25,405
Fresh drinking water came by aqueduct from the

128
00:04:25,405 --> 00:04:27,077
springs of Chapultepec,

129
00:04:27,077 --> 00:04:28,610
on the western shore.

130
00:04:28,960 --> 00:04:29,537
By 1519,

131
00:04:29,537 --> 00:04:33,001
Tenochtitlan may have had around two hundred thousand

132
00:04:33,001 --> 00:04:33,514
people,

133
00:04:33,514 --> 00:04:36,015
making it one of the largest cities in

134
00:04:36,015 --> 00:04:37,490
the world at the time.

135
00:04:37,840 --> 00:04:39,877
The Aztecs lived with the lake.

136
00:04:39,877 --> 00:04:41,023
They built on it,

137
00:04:41,023 --> 00:04:41,914
farmed on it,

138
00:04:41,914 --> 00:04:43,441
and traveled across it.

139
00:04:43,441 --> 00:04:45,860
What came next would do the opposite.

140
00:04:48,810 --> 00:04:49,526
In 1521,

141
00:04:49,526 --> 00:04:51,118
after a long siege,

142
00:04:51,118 --> 00:04:54,780
Spanish forces led by Hernán Cortés and their

143
00:04:54,780 --> 00:04:58,123
Indigenous allies conquered Tenochtitlan.

144
00:04:58,123 --> 00:05:00,670
The city was largely destroyed.

145
00:05:01,020 --> 00:05:01,209
The Spanish built their new capital right on

146
00:05:01,209 --> 00:05:01,284
top of the ruins.

147
00:05:01,284 --> 00:05:01,452
But they built it like a European city:

148
00:05:01,452 --> 00:05:01,565
streets instead of canals,

149
00:05:01,565 --> 00:05:01,670
carts instead of canoes.

150
00:05:02,020 --> 00:05:05,240
The dikes and canals that controlled the water

151
00:05:05,240 --> 00:05:06,062
fell apart.

152
00:05:06,062 --> 00:05:08,254
And during heavy rainy seasons,

153
00:05:08,254 --> 00:05:10,720
the lake rose and flooded the city.

154
00:05:11,070 --> 00:05:13,505
The worst flood began in 1629.

155
00:05:13,505 --> 00:05:17,118
Parts of the city stayed underwater for about

156
00:05:17,118 --> 00:05:18,060
five years.

157
00:05:18,410 --> 00:05:21,586
So the colonial government decided the lake was

158
00:05:21,586 --> 00:05:22,314
the enemy.

159
00:05:22,314 --> 00:05:24,100
If the valley had no exit,

160
00:05:24,100 --> 00:05:25,490
they would make one.

161
00:05:25,840 --> 00:05:27,326
After the great flood,

162
00:05:27,326 --> 00:05:30,751
there were even serious discussions about moving the

163
00:05:30,751 --> 00:05:32,431
capital to higher ground.

164
00:05:32,431 --> 00:05:33,206
In the end,

165
00:05:33,206 --> 00:05:34,305
the city stayed,

166
00:05:34,305 --> 00:05:36,760
and chose to fight the water instead.

167
00:05:37,110 --> 00:05:38,447
Starting in 1607,

168
00:05:38,447 --> 00:05:40,080
workers dug a tunnel,

169
00:05:40,080 --> 00:05:42,085
and later a huge open cut,

170
00:05:42,085 --> 00:05:44,907
through the hills in the north of the

171
00:05:44,907 --> 00:05:45,501
valley,

172
00:05:45,501 --> 00:05:48,323
to let water flow out toward the Gulf

173
00:05:48,323 --> 00:05:49,140
of Mexico.

174
00:05:49,490 --> 00:05:52,104
Drainage went on for centuries.

175
00:05:52,104 --> 00:05:52,840
In 1900,

176
00:05:52,840 --> 00:05:55,291
the Gran Canal was completed.

177
00:05:55,291 --> 00:05:55,781
Then,

178
00:05:55,781 --> 00:05:56,680
from 1975,

179
00:05:56,680 --> 00:06:00,520
deep drainage tunnels were dug far beneath the

180
00:06:00,520 --> 00:06:01,010
city.

181
00:06:01,360 --> 00:06:04,388
The first tunnel was designed by an engineer

182
00:06:04,388 --> 00:06:06,003
called Enrico Martínez.

183
00:06:06,003 --> 00:06:08,156
It often collapsed and clogged,

184
00:06:08,156 --> 00:06:10,848
and it took generations of work to turn

185
00:06:10,848 --> 00:06:12,530
it into a working drain.

186
00:06:12,880 --> 00:06:14,078
The Gran Canal,

187
00:06:14,078 --> 00:06:17,074
finished under President Porfirio Díaz,

188
00:06:17,074 --> 00:06:20,819
ran for about forty-seven kilometers to carry the

189
00:06:20,819 --> 00:06:23,290
valley's water and sewage north.

190
00:06:23,640 --> 00:06:24,969
Little by little,

191
00:06:24,969 --> 00:06:26,889
Lake Texcoco disappeared.

192
00:06:26,889 --> 00:06:27,406
Today,

193
00:06:27,406 --> 00:06:30,582
only small remnants of the old lake system

194
00:06:30,582 --> 00:06:31,320
are left.

195
00:06:31,670 --> 00:06:32,931
On the dry lakebed,

196
00:06:32,931 --> 00:06:34,444
the city grew and grew,

197
00:06:34,444 --> 00:06:36,777
especially in the twentieth century,

198
00:06:36,777 --> 00:06:39,550
when millions of people moved here from the

199
00:06:39,550 --> 00:06:40,370
countryside.

200
00:06:40,720 --> 00:06:41,376
In 1900,

201
00:06:41,376 --> 00:06:44,511
the city had around half a million people.

202
00:06:44,511 --> 00:06:45,167
By 2020,

203
00:06:45,167 --> 00:06:49,250
the metropolitan area had more than twenty-one million.

204
00:06:49,600 --> 00:06:51,863
And all those people needed water.

205
00:06:51,863 --> 00:06:53,091
The lake was gone.

206
00:06:53,091 --> 00:06:54,707
So the city looked down,

207
00:06:54,707 --> 00:06:56,000
under its own feet.

208
00:06:56,350 --> 00:06:57,876
If you're enjoying this,

209
00:06:57,876 --> 00:06:58,547
subscribe.

210
00:06:58,547 --> 00:07:00,927
Every week we find the story hidden in

211
00:07:00,927 --> 00:07:01,720
another map.

212
00:07:04,670 --> 00:07:07,311
Under the valley lies an aquifer:

213
00:07:07,311 --> 00:07:10,807
layers of sand and gravel soaked with water,

214
00:07:10,807 --> 00:07:13,370
like a giant underground sponge.

215
00:07:13,720 --> 00:07:15,919
From the late eighteen hundreds,

216
00:07:15,919 --> 00:07:18,651
and especially in the twentieth century,

217
00:07:18,651 --> 00:07:21,650
the city drilled thousands of wells into it.

218
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:23,745
As the water table dropped,

219
00:07:23,745 --> 00:07:26,424
wells had to be drilled deeper and deeper,

220
00:07:26,424 --> 00:07:28,355
often hundreds of meters down,

221
00:07:28,355 --> 00:07:30,100
to keep reaching the water.

222
00:07:30,450 --> 00:07:30,921
Today,

223
00:07:30,921 --> 00:07:33,745
most of the city's water still comes from

224
00:07:33,745 --> 00:07:34,216
wells,

225
00:07:34,216 --> 00:07:36,636
pumped from the aquifer beneath it.

226
00:07:36,636 --> 00:07:39,528
Estimates usually put it at around six out

227
00:07:39,528 --> 00:07:40,940
of every ten liters.

228
00:07:41,290 --> 00:07:43,006
The problem is balance.

229
00:07:43,006 --> 00:07:43,864
Every year,

230
00:07:43,864 --> 00:07:46,724
much more water is pumped out than rain

231
00:07:46,724 --> 00:07:47,940
can put back in.

232
00:07:48,290 --> 00:07:50,912
And the city makes refilling harder.

233
00:07:50,912 --> 00:07:54,031
Concrete and asphalt cover the old lakebed,

234
00:07:54,031 --> 00:07:56,937
so rain that once soaked into the ground

235
00:07:56,937 --> 00:07:58,780
now runs into the drains.

236
00:07:59,130 --> 00:08:00,874
Now remember that clay.

237
00:08:00,874 --> 00:08:03,853
The old lakebed clay is incredibly soft,

238
00:08:03,853 --> 00:08:05,596
and it's full of water.

239
00:08:05,596 --> 00:08:06,396
In places,

240
00:08:06,396 --> 00:08:08,430
it's more water than solid.

241
00:08:08,780 --> 00:08:11,820
When water is pumped from the layers below,

242
00:08:11,820 --> 00:08:14,168
water drains out of the clay too.

243
00:08:14,168 --> 00:08:16,586
And when the clay loses its water,

244
00:08:16,586 --> 00:08:18,313
it shrinks and compacts.

245
00:08:18,313 --> 00:08:20,040
The ground on top sinks.

246
00:08:20,390 --> 00:08:22,839
Think of a wet sponge under a heavy

247
00:08:22,839 --> 00:08:23,248
book.

248
00:08:23,248 --> 00:08:24,812
Squeeze the water out,

249
00:08:24,812 --> 00:08:26,786
and the sponge gets thinner.

250
00:08:26,786 --> 00:08:30,051
Except this sponge is the ground beneath twenty

251
00:08:30,051 --> 00:08:31,140
million people.

252
00:08:31,490 --> 00:08:33,221
And here's the worst part.

253
00:08:33,221 --> 00:08:35,016
Once the clay is compacted,

254
00:08:35,016 --> 00:08:36,555
it doesn't spring back.

255
00:08:36,555 --> 00:08:38,479
Even if the aquifer refilled,

256
00:08:38,479 --> 00:08:41,300
most of the lost height would never return.

257
00:08:41,650 --> 00:08:45,377
Scientists noticed the sinking more than a century

258
00:08:45,377 --> 00:08:45,743
ago.

259
00:08:45,743 --> 00:08:46,766
By the 1950s,

260
00:08:46,766 --> 00:08:50,201
engineers had clearly linked it to the pumping

261
00:08:50,201 --> 00:08:51,370
of groundwater.

262
00:08:54,320 --> 00:08:55,785
So how much has it sunk?

263
00:08:55,785 --> 00:08:57,835
Since the early twentieth century,

264
00:08:57,835 --> 00:09:00,120
some parts of the city have dropped by

265
00:09:00,120 --> 00:09:01,233
around ten meters.

266
00:09:01,233 --> 00:09:03,870
That's the height of a three-story building.

267
00:09:04,220 --> 00:09:07,671
Satellites now measure it with great precision.

268
00:09:07,671 --> 00:09:09,828
In the fastest-sinking areas,

269
00:09:09,828 --> 00:09:12,632
the ground is dropping by up to around

270
00:09:12,632 --> 00:09:15,220
thirty to forty centimeters a year.

271
00:09:15,570 --> 00:09:18,238
The measurements come from satellite radar,

272
00:09:18,238 --> 00:09:20,784
which can detect changes in the height of

273
00:09:20,784 --> 00:09:22,724
the ground of just millimeters,

274
00:09:22,724 --> 00:09:25,210
from hundreds of kilometers up in space.

275
00:09:25,560 --> 00:09:26,686
For comparison,

276
00:09:26,686 --> 00:09:29,219
global sea level is rising by a few

277
00:09:29,219 --> 00:09:30,626
millimeters a year.

278
00:09:30,626 --> 00:09:33,652
Parts of Mexico City are sinking roughly a

279
00:09:33,652 --> 00:09:35,200
hundred times faster.

280
00:09:35,550 --> 00:09:38,033
You can see it in the city's most

281
00:09:38,033 --> 00:09:39,275
famous monument.

282
00:09:39,275 --> 00:09:41,247
The Angel of Independence,

283
00:09:41,247 --> 00:09:42,343
built in 1910,

284
00:09:42,343 --> 00:09:44,388
stands on deep foundations.

285
00:09:44,388 --> 00:09:46,798
So as the street around it sank,

286
00:09:46,798 --> 00:09:48,770
the column seemed to rise.

287
00:09:49,120 --> 00:09:50,339
To reach its base,

288
00:09:50,339 --> 00:09:53,034
fourteen extra steps have had to be added

289
00:09:53,034 --> 00:09:54,060
over the years.

290
00:09:54,410 --> 00:09:55,519
In some places,

291
00:09:55,519 --> 00:09:58,846
metal well casings anchored deep in firm ground

292
00:09:58,846 --> 00:10:00,994
now stick out of the pavement,

293
00:10:00,994 --> 00:10:04,390
because the ground around them has dropped away.

294
00:10:04,740 --> 00:10:06,327
The Palace of Fine Arts,

295
00:10:06,327 --> 00:10:08,740
a huge building clad in heavy marble,

296
00:10:08,740 --> 00:10:11,279
has sunk noticeably since it was built.

297
00:10:11,279 --> 00:10:13,882
You can see that its entrance sits lower

298
00:10:13,882 --> 00:10:15,660
than the streets around it.

299
00:10:16,010 --> 00:10:17,851
The Metropolitan Cathedral,

300
00:10:17,851 --> 00:10:19,165
on the main square,

301
00:10:19,165 --> 00:10:22,518
began leaning as different parts of its foundation

302
00:10:22,518 --> 00:10:24,227
sank at different speeds.

303
00:10:24,227 --> 00:10:25,147
In the 1990s,

304
00:10:25,147 --> 00:10:28,762
engineers carefully removed soil from under the higher

305
00:10:28,762 --> 00:10:30,340
parts to straighten it.

306
00:10:30,690 --> 00:10:32,347
That's the real danger:

307
00:10:32,347 --> 00:10:33,452
uneven sinking.

308
00:10:33,452 --> 00:10:36,283
When one side of a building drops faster

309
00:10:36,283 --> 00:10:37,388
than the other,

310
00:10:37,388 --> 00:10:39,390
walls crack and floors tilt.

311
00:10:39,740 --> 00:10:42,799
Water pipes break as the ground shifts under

312
00:10:42,799 --> 00:10:43,206
them.

313
00:10:43,206 --> 00:10:44,090
Roads crack.

314
00:10:44,090 --> 00:10:47,556
And the metro and drainage tunnels must constantly

315
00:10:47,556 --> 00:10:48,440
be repaired.

316
00:10:48,790 --> 00:10:51,826
The soft clay also amplifies earthquakes.

317
00:10:51,826 --> 00:10:54,935
Seismic waves can shake much harder on the

318
00:10:54,935 --> 00:10:57,682
old lakebed than on solid rock just a

319
00:10:57,682 --> 00:10:59,200
few kilometers away.

320
00:10:59,550 --> 00:11:02,510
And the sinking isn't the same everywhere.

321
00:11:02,510 --> 00:11:05,332
It's fastest where the clay is thickest,

322
00:11:05,332 --> 00:11:07,534
on the old lakebed in the east.

323
00:11:07,534 --> 00:11:10,081
On the rocky hills around the edges,

324
00:11:10,081 --> 00:11:11,320
it's much slower.

325
00:11:14,270 --> 00:11:15,670
Here's the paradox.

326
00:11:15,670 --> 00:11:17,910
A city that was once a lake now

327
00:11:17,910 --> 00:11:20,360
suffers from two problems at once:

328
00:11:20,360 --> 00:11:20,920
floods,

329
00:11:20,920 --> 00:11:22,460
and not enough water.

330
00:11:22,810 --> 00:11:24,312
As the city sinks,

331
00:11:24,312 --> 00:11:26,605
its drains lose their slope.

332
00:11:26,605 --> 00:11:30,005
Water that once flowed downhill out of the

333
00:11:30,005 --> 00:11:32,851
valley now has to be pumped uphill,

334
00:11:32,851 --> 00:11:34,670
out of a growing bowl.

335
00:11:35,020 --> 00:11:38,192
So the city keeps building bigger tunnels.

336
00:11:38,192 --> 00:11:39,667
The Emisor Oriente,

337
00:11:39,667 --> 00:11:40,847
opened in 2019,

338
00:11:40,847 --> 00:11:44,830
is a drainage tunnel about sixty-two kilometers long.

339
00:11:45,180 --> 00:11:48,147
Much of the city's wastewater flows north,

340
00:11:48,147 --> 00:11:49,458
out of the valley,

341
00:11:49,458 --> 00:11:51,321
into the state of Hidalgo,

342
00:11:51,321 --> 00:11:54,080
where it has long been used to irrigate

343
00:11:54,080 --> 00:11:54,770
farmland.

344
00:11:54,770 --> 00:11:58,082
A huge treatment plant at Atotonilco now cleans

345
00:11:58,082 --> 00:11:58,910
part of it.

346
00:11:59,260 --> 00:11:59,958
Meanwhile,

347
00:11:59,958 --> 00:12:02,053
the wells can't supply everyone.

348
00:12:02,053 --> 00:12:04,783
So the city also brings water from outside

349
00:12:04,783 --> 00:12:05,545
the valley,

350
00:12:05,545 --> 00:12:07,450
through the Cutzamala System.

351
00:12:07,800 --> 00:12:11,172
Its reservoirs lie more than a hundred kilometers

352
00:12:11,172 --> 00:12:12,048
to the west.

353
00:12:12,048 --> 00:12:14,543
The water has to be pumped uphill by

354
00:12:14,543 --> 00:12:17,308
more than a thousand meters to reach the

355
00:12:17,308 --> 00:12:17,713
city.

356
00:12:17,713 --> 00:12:20,680
That takes enormous amounts of electricity.

357
00:12:21,030 --> 00:12:21,709
In 2024,

358
00:12:21,709 --> 00:12:23,520
after years of drought,

359
00:12:23,520 --> 00:12:26,992
the Cutzamala reservoirs fell to record lows,

360
00:12:26,992 --> 00:12:30,916
and many neighborhoods were left depending on water

361
00:12:30,916 --> 00:12:31,520
trucks.

362
00:12:31,870 --> 00:12:34,106
And a large share of the water is

363
00:12:34,106 --> 00:12:35,684
lost before it arrives.

364
00:12:35,684 --> 00:12:36,408
Old pipes,

365
00:12:36,408 --> 00:12:38,775
many cracked by the sinking ground,

366
00:12:38,775 --> 00:12:41,340
leak an estimated forty percent of it.

367
00:12:41,690 --> 00:12:43,890
That creates a vicious circle.

368
00:12:43,890 --> 00:12:45,665
Leaks mean more pumping.

369
00:12:45,665 --> 00:12:48,007
More pumping means more sinking.

370
00:12:48,007 --> 00:12:50,704
More sinking means more broken pipes,

371
00:12:50,704 --> 00:12:51,840
and more leaks.

372
00:12:52,190 --> 00:12:53,546
Every rainy season,

373
00:12:53,546 --> 00:12:56,191
huge amounts of rain still fall on the

374
00:12:56,191 --> 00:12:56,598
city.

375
00:12:56,598 --> 00:12:59,039
But most of it is sent away through

376
00:12:59,039 --> 00:12:59,853
the drains,

377
00:12:59,853 --> 00:13:02,430
while wells pump water up from below.

378
00:13:02,780 --> 00:13:04,191
During summer storms,

379
00:13:04,191 --> 00:13:06,564
heavy rain can fall in a short time,

380
00:13:06,564 --> 00:13:09,065
and streets on the lowest parts of the

381
00:13:09,065 --> 00:13:11,310
old lakebed still flood regularly.

382
00:13:14,260 --> 00:13:16,636
So can Mexico City stop sinking?

383
00:13:16,636 --> 00:13:17,788
Not completely,

384
00:13:17,788 --> 00:13:19,156
at least not soon.

385
00:13:19,156 --> 00:13:20,740
But it can slow down.

386
00:13:21,090 --> 00:13:24,046
The solutions are simple to describe and hard

387
00:13:24,046 --> 00:13:24,496
to do:

388
00:13:24,496 --> 00:13:25,974
fix the leaking pipes,

389
00:13:25,974 --> 00:13:27,774
pump less from the aquifer,

390
00:13:27,774 --> 00:13:30,216
and help more rain soak back into the

391
00:13:30,216 --> 00:13:30,730
ground.

392
00:13:31,080 --> 00:13:34,507
Some projects already collect rainwater from rooftops,

393
00:13:34,507 --> 00:13:38,058
especially in poorer neighborhoods that get little water

394
00:13:38,058 --> 00:13:39,180
from the network.

395
00:13:39,530 --> 00:13:43,018
Engineers are also studying ways to inject treated

396
00:13:43,018 --> 00:13:45,001
water back into the aquifer,

397
00:13:45,001 --> 00:13:47,941
to refill the giant sponge instead of only

398
00:13:47,941 --> 00:13:48,830
emptying it.

399
00:13:49,180 --> 00:13:50,514
And in Xochimilco,

400
00:13:50,514 --> 00:13:54,448
farmers still grow vegetables and flowers on chinampas,

401
00:13:54,448 --> 00:13:58,030
using the same technique the Aztecs used centuries

402
00:13:58,030 --> 00:13:58,381
ago.

403
00:13:58,381 --> 00:13:59,996
Living with the water,

404
00:13:59,996 --> 00:14:01,120
not against it.

405
00:14:01,470 --> 00:14:03,547
On part of the dry bed of Lake

406
00:14:03,547 --> 00:14:04,150
Texcoco,

407
00:14:04,150 --> 00:14:07,031
where a huge new airport was once planned,

408
00:14:07,031 --> 00:14:10,180
a large ecological park and wetlands are being

409
00:14:10,180 --> 00:14:10,850
restored.

410
00:14:11,200 --> 00:14:12,995
Mexico City isn't alone.

411
00:14:12,995 --> 00:14:16,010
Other cities on soft ground that pump too

412
00:14:16,010 --> 00:14:17,302
much groundwater,

413
00:14:17,302 --> 00:14:19,241
like Jakarta in Indonesia,

414
00:14:19,241 --> 00:14:21,610
are sinking for the same reason.

415
00:14:21,960 --> 00:14:24,886
That's why engineers around the world study Mexico

416
00:14:24,886 --> 00:14:25,977
City as a warning:

417
00:14:25,977 --> 00:14:28,215
this is what happens when a city takes

418
00:14:28,215 --> 00:14:30,739
much more water from the ground than nature

419
00:14:30,739 --> 00:14:31,600
can give back.

420
00:14:31,950 --> 00:14:35,002
But nowhere is the story clearer than here,

421
00:14:35,002 --> 00:14:37,359
in a city that turned a lake into

422
00:14:37,359 --> 00:14:37,776
land,

423
00:14:37,776 --> 00:14:39,787
and is now paying the price,

424
00:14:39,787 --> 00:14:41,590
centimeter by centimeter.

425
00:14:41,940 --> 00:14:44,150
So let's put the whole story together.

426
00:14:44,500 --> 00:14:47,670
Mexico City sits in a closed valley that

427
00:14:47,670 --> 00:14:49,835
was once filled with lakes.

428
00:14:49,835 --> 00:14:51,767
Over thousands of years,

429
00:14:51,767 --> 00:14:54,937
those lakes left behind a thick layer of

430
00:14:54,937 --> 00:14:55,401
soft,

431
00:14:55,401 --> 00:14:56,870
water-filled clay.

432
00:14:57,220 --> 00:14:59,186
Then the lake was drained,

433
00:14:59,186 --> 00:15:01,079
a giant city grew on top,

434
00:15:01,079 --> 00:15:04,573
and millions of people began pumping water from

435
00:15:04,573 --> 00:15:05,083
below.

436
00:15:05,083 --> 00:15:06,757
As the clay dries out,

437
00:15:06,757 --> 00:15:07,704
it compacts,

438
00:15:07,704 --> 00:15:09,160
and the city sinks.

439
00:15:09,510 --> 00:15:12,102
The Aztecs built a city that lived with

440
00:15:12,102 --> 00:15:12,750
the lake.

441
00:15:12,750 --> 00:15:14,435
Five hundred years later,

442
00:15:14,435 --> 00:15:15,602
the lake is gone.

443
00:15:15,602 --> 00:15:17,870
But the ground still remembers it.

444
00:15:18,220 --> 00:15:22,506
Every map hides a story like this one.

445
00:15:22,506 --> 00:15:26,683
If you want to discover the next one,

446
00:15:26,683 --> 00:15:29,870
subscribe to Timeline Earth.
